            answer: 'Monopolistic competition exhibits productive inefficiency in long-run equilibrium as firms do not produce at minimum AC. Instead, they produce on the downward-sloping portion of the AC curve where AC is still declining. Reasons for productive inefficiency: (1) Downward-sloping demand curve due to product differentiation, (2) Zero profit condition (P = AC) is satisfied before reaching minimum AC, (3) Tangency point of demand and AC curves occurs left of minimum AC, (4) Insufficient demand for each firm to expand to minimum efficient scale. Technical explanation: The demand curve becomes tangent to the AC curve at an output level below minimum AC. At this point, P = AC (zero economic profit) but AC could be lower at a higher output. If the firm produced at minimum AC, P would be below AC, causing losses. Implications: (1) Higher unit costs than technically necessary, (2) Excess capacity and underutilization of resources, (3) More firms and facilities than needed for given industry output. This contrasts with perfect competition where competitive pressure and horizontal demand force firms to minimum AC in long run. The productive inefficiency is the price paid for product variety.',
