            answer: 'The kinked demand curve model explains price rigidity in oligopoly based on asymmetric rival reactions to price changes. Model assumptions: If a firm raises price above current level: Rivals will not follow (keep their prices constant), so the firm loses substantial market share to competitors. Demand is relatively elastic above current price. If a firm lowers price below current level: Rivals will match the price cut to maintain market share, so the firm gains little market share. Demand is relatively inelastic below current price. Resulting demand curve: Kinked at current price P* - elastic (flat) above the kink, inelastic (steep) below the kink. The kink represents the current price-quantity combination. MR curve discontinuity: The corresponding MR curve has a vertical discontinuity (gap) at the kink output level. MC can shift within this discontinuous range without changing optimal price or quantity. This explains price rigidity - even with cost changes, the firm maintains price at P* as long as MC remains in the discontinuous range. Limitations: (1) Doesn\'t explain how P* was initially determined, (2) Assumes specific reaction patterns that may not hold, (3) Doesn\'t explain price leadership or coordination.',
