            question: 'Compare allocative efficiency across the four main market structures: perfect competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, and monopoly. Rank them from most to least efficient and explain why.',
            answer: 'Allocative efficiency ranking from most to least efficient: 1. Perfect Competition (Most efficient): Produces where P = MC in long run, achieving allocative efficiency. No deadweight loss. Resources allocated optimally with marginal benefit equal to marginal cost. Maximum total surplus. 2. Monopolistic Competition: P > MC in both short and long run due to downward-sloping demand. Small deadweight loss due to relatively elastic demand and competition from close substitutes. Gap between P and MC is moderate as firms have limited market power. 3. Oligopoly (varies by behavior): Competitive oligopoly: P > MC but extent depends on degree of competition. Deadweight loss moderate to large. Closer to monopolistic competition when competitive, closer to monopoly when collusive. Collusive oligopoly: Similar to monopoly with P >> MC and large deadweight loss as firms act as shared monopoly. 4. Monopoly (Least efficient): P >> MC with largest deadweight loss. Most severe allocative inefficiency as monopolist restricts output significantly below socially optimal level. Substantial foregone welfare due to underconsumption. Key insight: The greater the firm\'s market power (ability to set price above MC), the worse the allocative inefficiency. Competitive pressure improves allocative efficiency by constraining pricing power.',
