            question: 'Explain contestable markets theory. How can potential competition from hit-and-run entry discipline even monopolistic market structures? What conditions make markets contestable?',
            answer: 'Contestable markets theory suggests that potential competition can discipline firms even in concentrated markets, making entry threats as important as actual competition. Core concept: If entry and exit are costless, potential entrants can engage in hit-and-run entry - enter market, undercut incumbent prices to capture profits, then exit before incumbent responds. This threat constrains incumbent pricing even with monopoly structure. Key insight: Market structure (number of firms) may be less important than contestability (ease of entry/exit) in determining outcomes. How hit-and-run entry works: (1) Incumbent charges monopoly price above AC, earning supernormal profits, (2) Potential entrant identifies profit opportunity, (3) Entrant enters rapidly with lower price, capturing market share and profits, (4) Entrant exits before incumbent can respond or before retaliation, (5) Threat of this process forces incumbent to charge competitive prices even without actual entry. Conditions for contestable markets: (1) No sunk costs - all capital invested can be recovered on exit (reversible entry), (2) Free entry and exit - no legal, technical, or strategic barriers, (3) Fast entry and exit - hit-and-run possible before incumbent responds, (4) No incumbent advantages - entrants have access to same technology and costs. Examples: Airline routes with no hub investments (can redeploy planes), trucking services, some online platforms. Implications: Even single-firm markets can be competitive if perfectly contestable. Policy should focus on reducing entry barriers rather than just breaking up monopolies. Limitations: Pure contestability rarely exists - most industries have some sunk costs, incumbent advantages, and entry delays.',
